Every salon owner knows the dance. Phone buzzes mid-color application. It's a DM on Instagram — someone asking about a balayage. You can't stop. By the time you check, it's been 3 hours. The customer booked with someone else.

This is the central problem of running a modern salon: Instagram is now where 70-80% of new clients first reach out, but your stylists are physically with other clients all day. There's no front desk for DMs.

That gap is exactly where AI receptionists earn their keep.

## Why salons are different

Salon DMs aren't like restaurant DMs or e-commerce DMs. They have specific patterns:

- **Photo-first inquiries.** "Can you do this?" + screenshot of a Pinterest pin
- **Pricing questions before booking.** "How much for full highlights?"
- **Specific stylist requests.** "Is Maria available Saturday?"
- **Same-day urgency.** "Can you fit me in today??"
- **Long-form descriptions.** "I have curly hair, color-treated, last cut was…"

Generic chatbots fail at all of this. They give canned replies, miss the photo entirely, and never escalate properly. The result is a DM history full of "we'll get back to you" responses that drive customers crazy.

## What a properly trained salon AI handles

The right setup answers naturally and books real appointments:

- **Service pricing.** Customer asks "how much for highlights?" → AI gives the rate range based on hair length, says final quote requires a consult.
- **Stylist availability.** "Is Maria free Saturday?" → AI checks Maria's calendar specifically, offers 2-3 times.
- **Booking the appointment.** Customer picks a time → AI books it, sends confirmation, sets reminders.
- **Photo acknowledgment.** Customer sends a hair photo → AI says "Got the photo — Maria will review and confirm if she can match this. Booking your slot now to hold it."
- **Aftercare questions.** "How long should I wait before washing my hair?" → AI answers based on your aftercare guide.

## What it never tries to handle

Hand off these to a human (which means texting you, the owner):

- **Color formulation questions.** "Will my hair go orange?" — only a stylist can judge based on the actual hair.
- **Skin reaction concerns.** Anything medical — escalate.
- **Refund or complaint conversations.** Always to the owner.
- **Custom service requests** outside your menu.

The handoff is honest: "Let me have Maria check the photo and confirm she can match this — I'm holding your Saturday 2pm slot in the meantime."

## Real example DM flow

This is how a salon DM goes when the AI is set up correctly:

> **Customer (sends screenshot of ash blonde balayage):** "Hi! How much would something like this cost? Hair is currently dark brown, shoulder length."
>
> **AI:** "Beautiful look! Going from dark brown to that level of blonde is a multi-step process — usually 2 sessions, around $280-$340 per session depending on how your hair lifts. Want me to book a free consultation? It's 20 minutes — Maria assesses your hair and gives you a firm quote."
>
> **Customer:** "Sure! Saturday morning?"
>
> **AI:** "Saturday 11am or 12pm with Maria — which works?"
>
> **Customer:** "11am please"
>
> **AI:** "Booked. I'll send a text reminder Friday. One question: any color in your hair right now (highlights, henna, box dye)?"
>
> **Customer:** "Box dye about 6 months ago."
>
> **AI:** "Noted — Maria will factor that in. See you Saturday at 11."

That's a $300+ consultation booked at 9pm on a Tuesday while the owner was at dinner.

